 "title": "How the stage is worked out — and what 20 years of NSE data say",
 "data": "Weekly bars of liquid NSE stocks (₹1 Cr+ median daily turnover, price ₹20+), Jan 2006 – Oct 2026, every week of every stock still listed today. Returns are excess over the equal-weight average of the same liquid stocks, as plain averages; the days-in-Stage-2 table trims the 0.5% tails. 'Fell to Stage 4' = the engine showed Stage 4 within the next 26 weeks.",
 "rules": "Weekly chart, 30-week simple moving average of weekly closes (Mon–Fri weeks; this week's bar is the latest). The line is rising / falling when it is more than 1.5% above / below its value 6 weeks earlier, flat otherwise; price is above / below it when the weekly close is more than 2% over / under it. A stage remembers its range (highest weekly high, lowest weekly low since it began). Stage 1 → 2: close above the line, the line not falling, close above the base's high, base at least 6 weeks old. 2 → 3: once the line has risen, it stops rising and price closes under it, or it stays flat 4 weeks, or price closes more than 5% under it two weeks running even while it still rises. 3 → 4: close below the top's low, or below a falling line 2 weeks running. 4 → 1: once the line has fallen, it stops falling with price back at it 2 weeks running, or it stays flat 4 weeks, or price closes more than 8% over it two weeks running even while it still falls. A breakout that slips back under the line before the line has turned up is a failed breakout: the stock stays in the same base and its clock keeps running (the same for a failed breakdown from a top). Direct moves: 2 → 4 below a falling line; 3 → 2 above a rising line and the top's high, or 4 weeks above a rising line; 4 → 2 above a rising line and every high since the decline began. A stock that has not traded for 25 sessions is read afresh.",
