 "findings": [
  "The old rule (today's close against the 150-day line and its 20-day slope, with no memory of what came before) could not tell a base from a top. On weeks any chart reader would call a base — a flat 30-week line after a 30%+ fall — it said Stage 3 20% of the time, and it called 36% of clear tops Stage 1. The weekly engine gets 98% of those bases and 79% of those tops right.",
  "The old label changed 6.2 times a year per stock and 42% of its Stage 2 runs lasted 4 weeks or less. The weekly engine changes 2.3 times a year, 5% of its Stage 2 runs are that short (median run 25 weeks against 6), and 5.2% of its Stage 2 starts fall back to Stage 1 or 4 within 8 weeks.",
  "The labels mean more: over the next 6 months Stage 2 stocks beat the average liquid stock by +2.0% (old rule +1.8%), Stage 4 stocks trailed it by 2.8% (old 3.0%), and Stage 3 now marks tops (−0.4% over 6 months, −0.5% over a year). Both halves of the 20 years agree on Stage 2 and Stage 4 (2006–15: +1.4% and −2.5%; 2016–26: +2.2% and −2.9%).",
  "The engine waits for the breakout, as Weinstein does: on 10% of weeks where price had been above a rising line for 4 weeks it still said Stage 1, because the stock had not yet closed above its base's high (or the base was under 6 weeks old). Those weeks did no better than the average stock over the next 6 months (−0.4%), while the same picture after a breakout beat it by +2.5% (2006–15: −0.7% against +2.1%; 2016–26: −0.4% against +2.6%).",
  "A sharp break ends a stage early. Stage 2 weeks that sat more than 5% under a line that was still rising behaved like tops (−0.4% to −2.6% over the next 6 months, and 76–91% fell into Stage 4 within 6 months), so two such weekly closes now end Stage 2. Rallies up to 10% over a line that was still falling kept behaving like Stage 4 (−2.3% to −2.6%), so a Stage 4 ends early only after two weekly closes more than 8% over the line.",
  "Days in Stage 2, all stocks: bought in the first 40 trading days they were level with the average stock over the next 6 months (−0.4% to +0.1%); from day 41 to day 250 they beat it by +1.2% to +2.8%; after day 250, +0.8% (−0.6% over a year), and 50% fell into Stage 4 within 6 months.",
  "With an RS rating of 70+ the edge is there from the first day: +2.5% over 6 months and +6.0% over a year in the first 10 days, +3.5% to +3.9% over 6 months from day 41 to day 250, and mostly gone after day 250 (+0.9% over 6 months, −1.1% over a year, 46% fell into Stage 4). Both halves show it: the early edge was smaller in 2006–15 (+1.0% over 6 months) than in 2016–26 (+2.8%), and after day 250 the next year weakened in both (2006–15: +3.0% → +0.6%; 2016–26: +4.3% → −1.6%) while the share falling into Stage 4 rose (35% → 43%; 32% → 46%).",
  "For a swing trade of a few weeks the age hardly matters: over the next 4 weeks every age bucket up to day 500 was between −0.1% and +0.7% against the average stock. It matters for positional and investment holds. In every bucket fewer than half of the stocks beat the average stock (at most 48% over 6 months) — a few big winners lift the average — so the stop-loss still does the work.",
  "So: notice a stock the day it enters Stage 2 if its RS rating is 70+; otherwise let it prove itself for about 40 trading days (two months). Up to about 250 trading days (a year) is the window to buy; after that it is a mature Stage 2 — manage what you hold and be choosy with new buys. Weinstein's own advice points the same way: buy the breakout from the base or the first pullback, not late in the advance."
 ],
 "caveats": "Only stocks still listed today (delisted companies are missing, which if anything flatters late Stage 2). Weekly snapshots overlap, so these are averages of stock-weeks, not independent trades. Ages are counted as weeks × 5, close to trading days. Past results are evidence, not a promise."
